But I don't think you would have the same problem (unless we're talking about different things and I'm missing your point).

For example, if I implemented this interface like the below, the IDE/analyzers have been smart enough in my experience to not emit a warning as they understand that the argument is "used" by the interface declaration, even if it is never referenced in the method body.

```csharp
public interface IValidate<T>
{
    bool IsValid(T value);
}

public class AlwaysValid<T> : IValidate<T>
{
    public bool IsValid(T value)
    {
        // Don't care about value, YOLO
        return true;
    }
}
```
